---
/*
  Datenschutzerklaerung, ausformuliert am 07.09.2026.
  Neufassung, keine Uebersetzung. Gliederungslogik und Detailgrad orientieren
  sich an "/Users/manuelgonzalez/manuel-os/projects/websites/rechtstexte/datenschutz.md"
  (Growth Club GmbH), der Inhalt ist aber eigenstaendig auf die lettische
  KG Models SIA und auf die tatsaechlich eingesetzten Dienste zugeschnitten.

  Vorgabe Manuel vom 07.09.2026: lieber einen Dienst zu viel nennen als einen
  zu wenig. Genannt sind deshalb Render, Google Workspace, Instagram und Meta,
  das Creator-Portal wunderportal.com, Zernio, Infloww, Slack, WhatsApp,
  Discord und das Bewerbungsformular auf /apply.

  Zwei harte Punkte aus dem Konzept, die die Pruefung ueberstehen muessen:
  1. Die Empfaengerliste nennt nur Dienste, die wirklich Daten sehen.
  2. Eine hier genannte Speicherdauer muss ausgefuehrt werden, nicht nur
     gedruckt. Deshalb stehen konkrete Fristen als offener Punkt drin und
     nicht als Behauptung.

  Belegte Tatsachen zur Technik dieser Seite, geprueft am 07.09.2026:
  - Hosting laeuft ueber Render, siehe render.yaml in diesem Repo.
  - Die Seite laedt keine externen Schriften. Epilogue liegt lokal unter
    /fonts/epilogue-latin.woff2 und wird in src/layouts/Base.astro vorgeladen.
  - Es gibt kein Cookie, kein Pixel, keinen Consent-Dialog. Plausible ist in
    src/site.ts nur optional vorgesehen und ohne PUBLIC_PLAUSIBLE_DOMAIN aus.
  - Das Formular auf /apply postet an FORM_ENDPOINT und ist ohne diese
    Umgebungsvariable abgeschaltet.
  - Die MX-Eintraege von wundermgmt.com zeigen auf Google, per dig geprueft
    am 07.09.2026. Daher Google Workspace als Mail- und Ablagesystem.
*/
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { legal } from '../site';

/*
  Empfaengeruebersicht. Anschriften nur dort, wo sie belegt sind. Wo der Sitz
  des Anbieters nicht sauber belegt ist, steht das als offener Punkt und wird
  nicht geraten.
*/
const recipients = [
  {
    purpose: 'Hosting and delivery of this website, server log files',
    who: 'Render Services, Inc., United States',
    basis: 'Processor, standard contractual clauses',
    open: 'Registered address and processing region to be confirmed',
  },
  {
    purpose: 'Email, calendar, documents and file storage',
    who: 'Google Ireland Limited, Gordon House, Barrow Street, Dublin 4, Ireland, with Google LLC, United States',
    basis: 'Processor',
    open: 'Processing agreement reference to be added',
  },
  {
    purpose: 'Our profiles, creator accounts and reach data on Instagram',
    who: 'Meta Platforms Ireland Limited, 4 Grand Canal Square, Grand Canal Harbour, Dublin 2, Ireland',
    basis: 'Art. 6(1)(f) and Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR, joint controllership for profile insights',
    open: 'Joint controller arrangement under Art. 26 GDPR to be assessed',
  },
  {
    purpose: 'Scheduled publishing to Instagram through an authorised interface',
    who: 'Zernio',
    basis: 'Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR, processor where applicable',
    open: 'Legal name, seat and processing agreement to be confirmed',
  },
  {
    purpose: 'OnlyFans chat and vault management through an authorised interface',
    who: 'Infloww',
    basis: 'Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR, processor where applicable',
    open: 'Legal name, seat and processing agreement to be confirmed',
  },
  {
    purpose: 'Creator portal at wunderportal.com, login and performance figures',
    who: `${legal.company}, operating the portal, with its hosting and database provider`,
    basis: 'Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR',
    open: 'Hosting and database provider behind the portal to be named here',
  },
  {
    purpose: 'Internal coordination and, where agreed, coordination with creators',
    who: 'Slack Technologies Limited, Salesforce Tower, North Dock, Dublin, Ireland, Salesforce group',
    basis: 'Art. 6(1)(b) and Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR',
    open: 'Processing agreement reference to be added',
  },
  {
    purpose: 'Messenger contact with creators who choose that channel',
    who: 'WhatsApp Ireland Limited, 4 Grand Canal Square, Grand Canal Harbour, Dublin 2, Ireland, with WhatsApp LLC, United States',
    basis: 'Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR',
    open: null,
  },
  {
    purpose: 'Team and production coordination in our own server',
    who: 'Discord, Inc., United States',
    basis: 'Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR, and Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR where the exchange is part of the work',
    open: 'Registered address and transfer instrument to be confirmed',
  },
  {
    purpose: 'Bookkeeping, tax advice and legal advice',
    who: 'Advisers bound by professional secrecy, in Latvia',
    basis: 'Art. 6(1)(c) and Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR',
    open: null,
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Privacy | Wunder"
  description="What data this site collects, who receives it, on what legal basis and how long it is kept."
  noindex
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap wrap-narrow">
      <h1>Privacy</h1>

      <p class="draft-note">
        Draft wording, complete but not legally reviewed. Every item marked TO BE COMPLETED BY
        COUNSEL has to be filled in, and the page as a whole has to be confirmed, before it goes
        live. This text was written from scratch. The old generator template from the previous site
        is deliberately not carried over.
      </p>

      <h2>1. Who is responsible</h2>
      <p>
        {legal.company}<br />
        {legal.street}<br />
        {legal.city}<br />
        {legal.country}<br />
        Registration number {legal.regNumber}
      </p>
      <p>
        Represented by Manuel Gonzalez, CEO, and Lauris Kalnins, COO. Wunder is a brand name of this
        company. There is no second entity behind it.
      </p>
      <p>
        For anything about your data, a plain message is enough:
        <a href={`mailto:${legal.privacyEmail}`}>{legal.privacyEmail}</a>. For everything else,
        <a href={`mailto:${legal.contactEmail}`}>{legal.contactEmail}</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="todo">
        TO BE COMPLETED BY COUNSEL: whether this company has to appoint a data protection officer
        under Latvian law and, if so, the contact details. Also whether a representative under
        Art. 27 GDPR is needed for any activity carried out from outside the European Union.
      </p>

      <h2>2. What this policy covers</h2>
      <p>
        It covers this website, the application form on our apply page, contact with us by email or
        messenger, and the work with creators once we start, including the creator portal at
        wunderportal.com and the tools listed in section 7.
      </p>
      <p>
        It does not cover the platforms themselves. What OnlyFans or Instagram do with your data as
        their own user is governed by their own terms, and we have no say in it.
      </p>

      <h2>3. Visiting this website</h2>

      <h3>3.1 Server log files</h3>
      <p>
        Every time a page here is requested, the hosting provider processes the technical data it
        needs in order to serve the page:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>the IP address of the requesting device</li>
        <li>date and time of the request</li>
        <li>the file requested, the amount of data transferred and whether the request succeeded</li>
        <li>browser type and version, operating system and, where the browser sends it, the referring page</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The legal basis is Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR. Our legitimate interest is a site that works and stays
        secure. We do not merge these logs with other data and we do not analyse them per person.
        Hosting runs on Render Services, Inc., United States. The site is delivered as static files.
      </p>
      <p class="todo">
        TO BE COMPLETED BY COUNSEL: the processing agreement with the hosting provider, the exact log
        retention period the provider applies, and the processing region.
      </p>

      <h3>3.2 No cookies, no tracking, no consent banner</h3>
      <p>
        This site sets no cookies. There is no tracking pixel, no session recording, no heatmap, no
        cross site identifier and no advertising tag. That is also why there is no consent banner
        here asking you to agree to any of it. A banner would be theatre if there is nothing behind
        it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The site loads no fonts from an external service either. The typefaces are served from our
        own domain, so visiting a page does not open a connection to a font provider. The same is
        true for icons and images.
      </p>

      <h3>3.3 Statistics, if we ever switch them on</h3>
      <p>
        The build can optionally use Plausible, a cookieless statistics tool that measures page views
        in aggregate and sets no identifier on your device. It is switched off in this version. If we
        switch it on, we will describe it here first, with the provider, the data and the legal
        basis. Until this section says it is running, it is not running.
      </p>

      <h2>4. The application form</h2>
      <p>
        On our apply page you can send us a short application. The fields are: your Instagram handle,
        a link to your OnlyFans or link page, the country where you pay tax, whether you are
        currently under contract and until when, a revenue band, a publishing frequency band, your
        email address, and an optional free text note.
      </p>
      <p>
        We do not ask for identity documents, photographs, bank details or login credentials, and the
        form accepts no file uploads at all. Please do not send us special categories of data under
        Art. 9 GDPR, for example health data, through this form.
      </p>
      <p>
        What happens with a submission: it is delivered to a Wunder mailbox and read there by the
        people who decide whether a call makes sense. The mailbox runs on Google Workspace, see
        section 5. The form contains one hidden field that real people never fill in. It is there to
        stop automated submissions without handing your data to a third party bot protection service.
      </p>
      <p>
        The legal basis is Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR, because you are asking us to take steps before a
        possible agreement. Where a submission does not lead to an agreement, the basis for keeping
        it for a short period is Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR, our legitimate interest in being able to
        reconstruct who we answered and why.
      </p>
      <p class="todo">
        TO BE COMPLETED BY COUNSEL: the retention period for applications that do not lead to an
        agreement, and the deletion routine that actually executes it. A period printed here without
        a real deletion job behind it is worse than none. Also to be confirmed: the final form
        endpoint and whether any provider sits between the form and the mailbox.
      </p>

      <h2>5. Contacting us, email and office systems</h2>
      <p>
        If you write to us, we process what you send in order to answer. The legal basis is
        Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR where the message concerns an agreement or the run up to one, otherwise
        Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR, our legitimate interest in answering the people who write to us.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our mailboxes, calendars, documents and file storage run on Google Workspace. The provider
        for users in the European Economic Area is Google Ireland Limited, Gordon House, Barrow
        Street, Dublin 4, Ireland, with processing by Google LLC in the United States. Your contact
        and agreement data are processed there to the extent they appear in messages, appointments or
        documents.
      </p>
      <p class="todo">
        TO BE COMPLETED BY COUNSEL: the reference to the processing agreement with Google and the
        data regions configured for this workspace.
      </p>

      <h2>6. The creator portal</h2>
      <p>
        Creators we work with get an account for the portal at wunderportal.com. It shows the figures
        from the accounts we manage for you, the current state of the work and the documents that
        belong to the collaboration.
      </p>
      <p>
        Processed there are your login identity, your name and email address, the performance figures
        pulled from the platforms we manage for you, and what you upload or agree there. Your portal
        identity is separate from the staff system and can never hold a staff role. The legal basis
        is Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR, because the portal is part of what we agreed to deliver.
      </p>
      <p class="todo">
        TO BE COMPLETED BY COUNSEL: the hosting and database provider behind the portal has to be
        named here by legal name, seat and transfer instrument, and the processing agreement has to
        be referenced. The portal is described here, its infrastructure is not documented yet.
      </p>

      <h2>7. The tools we use while we work with you</h2>
      <p>
        These are the systems that actually see data when we run an account. Access always runs
        through an interface you authorised and can withdraw. You do not hand us a password.
      </p>

      <h3>7.1 Zernio</h3>
      <p>
        Instagram publishing runs through Zernio, an authorised interface with server side
        scheduling. Processed there are the account we publish to, the posts and captions we prepare,
        the publishing times and the reach figures the interface returns. The legal basis is
        Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR.
      </p>
      <p class="todo">
        TO BE COMPLETED BY COUNSEL: legal name, seat, transfer instrument and processing agreement
        for this provider.
      </p>

      <h3>7.2 Infloww</h3>
      <p>
        OnlyFans chat and vault management runs through Infloww, also an authorised interface.
        Processed there are the messages exchanged with your fans, the media in your vault, prices
        and offers, and the figures the interface reports back. Fan data appear in this processing
        because a conversation has two sides. The legal basis is Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR for you as our
        contract partner, and Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR for the handling of fan messages, with our legitimate
        interest in running the account we were engaged to run.
      </p>
      <p class="todo">
        TO BE COMPLETED BY COUNSEL: legal name, seat, transfer instrument and processing agreement
        for this provider, and the roles of creator, agency and platform in relation to fan data.
        This is the part of the setup with the highest exposure and it needs a proper assessment,
        not a sentence.
      </p>

      <h3>7.3 Instagram and Meta</h3>
      <p>
        We run our own profiles and, where agreed, we publish on the creator accounts we manage. When
        you visit or interact with a profile, Meta processes your data on its own responsibility. The
        provider for users in the European Economic Area is Meta Platforms Ireland Limited, 4 Grand
        Canal Square, Grand Canal Harbour, Dublin 2, Ireland.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the aggregated statistics about the use of a profile, we are joint controllers with Meta
        under Art. 26 GDPR. The legal basis for our part is Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR, our legitimate
        interest in knowing whether what we publish reaches anyone, and Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR where the
        account belongs to a creator we manage. You can exercise your rights against us and against
        Meta.
      </p>
      <p>
        Links from this site to a social profile are ordinary links. Data reach the provider only
        when you click one.
      </p>
      <p class="todo">
        TO BE COMPLETED BY COUNSEL: the joint controller arrangement for profile insights, and
        whether paid advertising is switched on. No advertising pixel runs on this site today, and if
        one is added it needs its own section and a consent mechanism before it goes live.
      </p>

      <h3>7.4 Slack</h3>
      <p>
        Internal coordination, and where agreed the coordination with creators, runs on Slack. The
        provider for users in the European Economic Area is Slack Technologies Limited, Salesforce
        Tower, North Dock, Dublin, Ireland, part of the Salesforce group. Processed are your name,
        your email address and the content of the messages. The legal basis is Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR
        where the exchange is part of the work, otherwise Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR, our legitimate interest
        in an orderly internal process.
      </p>

      <h3>7.5 WhatsApp</h3>
      <p>
        Some creators prefer WhatsApp for day to day contact. The provider for users in the European
        Economic Area is WhatsApp Ireland Limited, 4 Grand Canal Square, Grand Canal Harbour,
        Dublin 2, Ireland, part of the Meta group, with transfers to WhatsApp LLC in the United
        States. Processed are your mobile number, your profile name and the content of your messages.
        Message content is end to end encrypted, connection and usage data are not.
      </p>
      <p>
        Using this channel is voluntary. If you would rather not, everything runs by email or through
        the portal instead, at no disadvantage to you. Please do not send special categories of data
        under Art. 9 GDPR this way. The legal basis is Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR, because the exchange is
        part of the work we agreed.
      </p>

      <h3>7.6 Discord</h3>
      <p>
        Parts of the team and the production coordination run in our own Discord server. The provider
        is Discord, Inc., United States. Processed are your account name, your profile and the
        content of the messages in the channels you are in. The legal basis is Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR, our
        legitimate interest in coordinated production, and Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR where the exchange is
        part of the work agreed with you.
      </p>
      <p class="todo">
        TO BE COMPLETED BY COUNSEL: registered address, transfer instrument and whether a processing
        agreement is available for this provider.
      </p>

      <h2>8. Job applications</h2>
      <p class="todo">
        TO BE COMPLETED BY COUNSEL: the careers path runs on a separate site and a separate system
        and has to be described separately, with its own fields, its own readers, its own recipients
        and its own retention period. Creator applications under section 4 and job applications are
        not the same processing and must not be described in one paragraph.
      </p>

      <h2>9. Who receives your data</h2>
      <p>
        We pass data on only as far as the purposes above require. The list names the services that
        actually see data. It is not a precautionary list of everything on the market, and it is not
        shorter than reality either.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Purpose</th>
              <th scope="col">Recipient and seat</th>
              <th scope="col">Basis</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              recipients.map((row) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{row.purpose}</td>
                  <td>
                    {row.who}
                    {row.open && <span class="todo"><br />TO BE COMPLETED BY COUNSEL: {row.open}.</span>}
                  </td>
                  <td>{row.basis}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Beyond this, we pass data on where the law obliges us to, or where it is necessary to
        establish, exercise or defend legal claims.
      </p>

      <h2>10. Transfers outside the European Union</h2>
      <p>
        Some of the providers above are based in the United States or process data there. That
        applies to Render for the hosting, to Google, to Meta including WhatsApp, to Slack through
        the Salesforce group, and to Discord.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where a provider is certified under the EU US Data Privacy Framework, the transfer rests on
        the adequacy decision of the European Commission. In every other case, and in addition, we
        rely on standard contractual clauses under Art. 46(2)(c) GDPR, together with the technical
        measures the providers apply, in particular encryption in transit and at rest and restricted
        access rights.
      </p>
      <p>
        Even with those measures, it cannot be ruled out that authorities in the United States access
        data, and that the legal remedies available to you there are weaker than in the European
        Union. You can check whether a specific provider is currently certified in the public list at
        dataprivacyframework.gov.
      </p>
      <p class="todo">
        TO BE COMPLETED BY COUNSEL: per provider, the current certification status and the transfer
        instrument actually in place, plus a transfer impact assessment where one is required. Also
        to be recorded: where the team working on the accounts is located, and what instrument covers
        that.
      </p>

      <h2>11. Legal bases at a glance</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR, performance of an agreement or steps taken before entering into one:
          your application, the work on your accounts, the portal, the exchange over email and
          messenger.
        </li>
        <li>
          Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR, legitimate interest: the technical operation and security of this site,
          answering enquiries that are not about an agreement, internal coordination, and the reach
          figures of our own profiles.
        </li>
        <li>
          Art. 6(1)(a) GDPR, consent: only where we ask you for it separately and you can withdraw
          it at any time with effect for the future, Art. 7(3) GDPR. Nothing on this site currently
          runs on consent, because nothing on it requires any.
        </li>
        <li>
          Art. 6(1)(c) GDPR, legal obligation: keeping records that tax and commercial law require us
          to keep.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>12. How long we keep data</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Server log files: short term, within the retention the hosting provider applies.</li>
        <li>
          Applications that do not lead to an agreement: kept only as long as we need them to answer
          and to reconstruct our decision.
        </li>
        <li>
          Data from a running collaboration, including messages: for the term of the agreement and
          afterwards for as long as we need them to settle it and to defend legal claims.
        </li>
        <li>
          Agreements, invoices and accounting records: for the statutory retention periods under
          Latvian tax and accounting law.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="todo">
        TO BE COMPLETED BY COUNSEL: the concrete period for each of these categories, and the
        statutory retention periods that apply to this company in Latvia. Every period stated here
        has to be backed by a deletion routine that runs. We would rather publish this section late
        than publish a period we do not execute.
      </p>

      <h2>13. Your rights</h2>
      <p>You have the right:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>to information about the data we hold about you, Art. 15 GDPR</li>
        <li>to have inaccurate data corrected, Art. 16 GDPR</li>
        <li>to erasure, Art. 17 GDPR</li>
        <li>to restriction of processing, Art. 18 GDPR</li>
        <li>to data portability, Art. 20 GDPR</li>
        <li>
          to object to processing based on legitimate interest, Art. 21 GDPR, on grounds relating to
          your particular situation, and at any time and without reasons where the processing is for
          direct marketing
        </li>
        <li>to withdraw a consent you gave, at any time with effect for the future, Art. 7(3) GDPR</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A plain message to <a href={`mailto:${legal.privacyEmail}`}>{legal.privacyEmail}</a> is
        enough. We do not require a particular form, and we do not charge for it.
      </p>

      <h2>14. Complaints to a supervisory authority</h2>
      <p>
        You can complain to a data protection supervisory authority, in particular in the country
        where you live or work. The authority responsible for us is the Latvian Data State
        Inspectorate, Datu valsts inspekcija, in Riga.
      </p>
      <p class="todo">
        TO BE COMPLETED BY COUNSEL: the current postal address, telephone number and website of the
        Latvian Data State Inspectorate, confirmed at the time of publication.
      </p>

      <h2>15. Do you have to give us your data</h2>
      <p>
        Providing personal data is neither required by law nor by contract. Without the entries in
        the application form, though, we cannot assess an application, and without the data needed to
        run an account, we cannot run it.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is no automated decision making, including profiling, within the meaning of
        Art. 22 GDPR. A person reads every application and a person decides.
      </p>

      <h2>16. Changes to this policy</h2>
      <p>
        We update this policy when the law changes or when we change the tools we use. The version
        published here is the one that applies. Version of 7 September 2026, not yet legally
        reviewed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .draft-note {
    padding: 16px 20px;
    border: 1px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--accent-soft);
    color: var(--text);
    max-width: 68ch;
    margin-bottom: 40px;
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: 22px;
    margin-top: 44px;
  }

  h3 {
    font-size: 18px;
    text-transform: none;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0;
    margin-top: 28px;
  }

  .todo {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .table-scroll {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin: 24px 0 32px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--surface);
  }

  table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 640px;
    font-size: 15px;
  }

  th,
  td {
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
    padding: 12px 14px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  th {
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  td {
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  tbody tr:last-child td {
    border-bottom: none;
  }
</style>
